s": [{"content": "Let's look up for more Human-Readable Medium", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Take Away", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"type": "post_instructions", "contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_skills", "contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}]}]}], "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}]}], "type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Human-Readable Medium", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 6, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.2", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about a human-readable medium.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Understand key information about the concepts, principles, and methodologies in simple spoken and written situations.  Talk about programming techniques, use cases, kinds, and sequences in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures.  Identifies general aspects related to Software Programming in short written or spoken conversations about past experiences.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob06re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "computing", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the use of a computer to process data", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I was reading about human-readable medium in computing. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob06aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "relevant", "type": "name"}, {"content": "Important, necessary", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Read about the topic and take notes of the most relevant ideas.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob06aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "barcode", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the series of identification lines printed in most of the products ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: For example, those machines to read the barcodes show us human-readable information hidden in the bars.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob06aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "support", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to help, to assist", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Read about the topic and take notes of the most relevant ideas, don't copy literary from the source, support your ideas.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob06aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "be able to", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to be capable of doing something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The author explains that human-readable media is a term we use not only to refer to all the data and information humans are able to understand, but also refers to short data strings that are easy to remember. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob06aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "codify", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to systematize", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I don't understand why we cannot codify all the information into human-readable representations.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob06aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Search for more information about Human-Readable Medium. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write down a short paragraph about a device that helps humans read software codifications.  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Use the grammar covered in this lesson. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share the information with your partners.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I search for information about Human-Readable Medium ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write a paragraph about human-readable medium.  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I use imperative sentences, and the simple past to write down my own ideas. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share my ideas with my partners.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Imperatives to Give Instructions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Use <strong>imperative sentence</strong> when you want to instruct someone to do something.  ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "An <b>imperative</b> is a type of sentence that omits the subject and keeps the verb in its base form to give commands, orders, and instructions. </br></br>  For example: </br> <strong><i>Stand</strong> up! </br> <strong>Listen</strong> to me!</i> </br></br>  You can also make an <b>imperative sentence</b> negative  to prevent someone from doing something; to make a <strong> negative imperative</strong>, start the sentence with the auxiliary verb <strong>do</strong> and the negative particle <strong>not</strong>; you can also use the contraction <strong>don't</strong> and the base form of the verb.</br></br>  For example: <i></br> <strong>Don't</strong> open that file! </br> <strong>Don't</strong> translate that code! </i>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I wrote some sentences about the topic, look!", "type": "example"}, {"content": " Read about the topic and take notes of the most relevant ideas, don't copy literary from the source, support your ideas.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Simple Past of Verb to Be in Negative and Questions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the <b>simple past</b> to express actions that started and finished in the past.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Remember that we use the verb <span class=\"strong\">be</span> to talk about states, emotions, and locations; we can also use the past of the verb <span class=\"strong\">be</span> to express the same ideas in the past. The past form of the verb <span class=\"strong\">be</span> is <span class=\"strong\">was</span> for singular nouns and <span class=\"strong\">were</span> for plural nouns. <br/> <br/>    Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">was</span> able to read those difficult codes. <br/> We <span class=\"strong\">were</span> happy about the new software. </span>  <br/><br/>  We follow the same rules as in the present to make negative and interrogative statements in the past; if we want to make a negative statement, we add the particle <span class=\"strong\">not</span> to the verb <span class=\"strong\">be</span>; we can also use the contractions <span class=\"strong\">wasn't</span> and <span class=\"strong\">weren't</span>.<br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\">We <span class=\"strong\">weren't</span> good at reading those codes. <br/> I <span class=\"strong\">wasn't</span> comfortable with the new software.  </span><br/><br/>  To make questions, we need to put the verb <span class=\"strong\">be</span> before the subject. Remember that we can also use <span class=\"strong\">Wh words</span> to ask open questions. <br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">Were</span> able to read those codes? <br/> <span class=\"strong\">Why was</span> that so difficult to read? </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Were you in the software programming class? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "There wasn't enough information in the library about codification.", "type": "example"}, {"content": " What was the title of the book you read?", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Tips for Beginner Writers", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "When writing a text it's important to consider the <strong>grammar</strong> and <strong>punctuation rules</strong>, but remember that there are no rules for a writing process, each person has his/ her own voice and criteria.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Let's check some general <strong>tips for beginner writers</strong>: </br></br>  1.\t<strong>Plan</strong> your writing before you start, you can create a mind map to organize your ideas. </br> 2.\tMake a frame for your text; decide on the ideas you want to tackle in each paragraph, including the opening and closing ones. </br> 3.\tTry to <strong>avoid irrelevant storylines</strong>; write just what is relevant for the story. </br> 4.\tTry to <strong>be clear and specific</strong> when expressing your ideas. </br>5.\t<strong>Use connectors</strong> between ideas and paragraphs, so you give coherence to your text. </br> 6.\tTry not to overuse certain words. \u201cJust\u201d, \u201conly\u201d, \u201cagain\u201d, \u2018\u201dand\u201d, and \u201calso\u201d. </br> 7.\t<strong>Be coherent</strong> in the verbal time. Is the story set in the past, present, or future?   </br> 8.\t<strong>Proofread your text</strong> to correct possible mistakes, and make sure your text is clear for everyone.", "type": "text"}]}]}